ace only; since the service binds solely to the private IP, this does not create actual exposure but is broader than strictly necessary. No destructive actions, no abandoned/failed services, and package-health/failed-services checks show no post-executor regressions.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b43c4b66ba0547bf","cmd-2749baf7693b4c69","cmd-a5020c61e7ba46c2","cmd-313e96303a7347a9","cmd-2217e068115c44de","cmd-bc71665285354369","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"The executor correctly identified the pre-wired private-link NICs (node1 eth1 / node2 enp8s0), configured static addressing, moved node2's HTTP service to bind only to that private address, and published it through a DNAT redirect on node1 so that node3 can reach it via node1's management IP on port 80 — all verified end-to-end with curl from node3, plus a negative test confirming node2's service is unreachable directly. The executor additionally performed the mandatory sequential restart of all three nodes, capturing pre-restart boot identity and healthy state, a restart acknowledgement, a changed post-restart boot identity, and full re-verification of interface, firewall, service, and end-to-end publish-path state for each node; these boot ids are corroborated by the fixed after-executor lifecycle snapshot. All three material task outcomes are satisfied with direct, coherent evidence.","limitations":["The executor's own labels for interfaces/network ('priv') do not map to the authored environment's declared network name ('lan'), but the underlying NIC pairing and isolation properties match the intended topology, so this is a naming difference rather than a functional gap.","One node1 reboot attempt (cmd-b43c4b66ba0547bf) appears to have had no effect based on unchanged boot_id/uptime in following checks; this is treated as an unsuccessful command rather than a state change, per evaluation guidance, but is noted for completeness.","firew